  "narrative": "All proposed estimates should be interpreted using the registered signed-estimate convention. For claims with direction -1, a positive reported estimate means the raw exposed-minus-comparator contrast is negative, so the exposed cell has more negative dependency scores and therefore stronger dependency. For claims with direction +1, a positive reported estimate means the exposed cell has less negative scores and therefore weaker dependency. A clinically meaningful result should be large relative to each outcome's score distribution and should have an interval excluding zero with a lower bound beyond a small or trivial shift. These remain association tests in the supplied lung CRISPR dataset only, not causal or clinically generalizable claims.\n\nThe first block refines the accepted KEAP1-loss association with weaker NFE2L2 dependency by KRAS-mutation status. KEAP1 loss and KRAS mutation are common in lung adenocarcinoma biology, so if the large reversed effect persists in both KRAS-mutant and KRAS-wildtype strata, the association is less likely to be confined to one driver background. If it collapses or reverses in one stratum, the main effect would need a narrower context interpretation.\n\nThe second block refines the accepted SMARCA4-loss association with stronger SMARCA2 dependency by KRAS-mutation status. H6 and related strata are already very large and independently validated, so these KRAS-context tests ask whether the effect is robust across two major oncogenic-driver backgrounds. Plausible alternatives include context dilution, co-alteration confounding, or persistence of a broad chromatin-dependency effect.\n\nThe third block refines the accepted PTEN-loss association with stronger STAT3 dependency by KRAS-mutation status. PTEN loss has been robust in TP53-intact, TP53-loss, PIK3CA-intact, PIK3CA-mutant, and Avana-library strata. KRAS-mutant and KRAS-wildtype strata test whether another common driver background modifies the effect.\n\nThe fourth block completes the MSI-high-WRN context map in TP53-loss lines. The main MSI-high effect is huge and independently validated, and the TP53-intact stratum is accepted, but the TP53-loss cell is smaller. This is a robustness and cell-size stress test rather than a discovery of a new effect.\n\nThe fifth block adds focused mechanistic coverage for RPL11. MYC amplification can increase translational and ribosomal stress, potentially producing stronger RPL11 dependency preferentially when TP53 is intact. The full-sample MYC-RPL11 relationship is not promising enough to prioritize broadly, but the TP53-intact context is a more specific p53-dependent hypothesis.\n\nThe last three proposals test pathway-bypass and co-dependency logic around RAS and receptor tyrosine kinase dependencies. PIK3CA mutation may reduce KRAS dependency through downstream PI3K activation, so the registered direction is weaker KRAS dependency. STK11 loss in KRAS-mutant lines may reflect metabolic stress and co-mutational biology, possibly altering KRAS dependency in either direction. KRAS mutation may reduce ERBB2 dependency because mutually exclusive or bypass driver programs can substitute for HER2 signaling. These are exploratory associations; null, small, or reversed results would be acceptable and informative.\n\nI closed or deferred low-priority old active claims related to EGFR amplification, RB1 loss, ERBB2 interactions, redundant ARID1A/PIK3CA interaction tests, and other broad MYC or pathway candidates that have either failed validation, been superseded by stronger stratum evidence, or remain unlikely to yield meaningful differences. This preserves analysis capacity for accepted-effect subtype robustness, KRAS-context robustness, batch_a checks if still desired, and the new focused mechanistic tests.",
  "research_notes": "Round-5 explore update. Signed convention: direction -1 positive signed estimate = more negative dependency score = stronger dependency; direction +1 positive signed estimate = less negative score = weaker dependency. Accepted/validated central associations: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency; PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency accepted on discovery and robust stratum evidence, though H13 lacks its own independent validation. New priority tests: KEAP1-NFE2L2 by KRAS-mutant/wildtype; SMARCA4-SMARCA2 by KRAS-mutant/wildtype; PTEN-STAT3 by KRAS-mutant/wildtype; MSI-WRN in TP53-loss; MYC-RPL11 in TP53-intact; PIK3CA to weaker KRAS dependency; STK11 in KRAS-mutant to KRAS dependency; KRAS mutation to weaker ERBB2 dependency. Keep existing subtype robustness active: H14 SMARCA2, H15 WRN, H63 NFE2L2, H64 STAT3. Keep batch_a checks H58-H61 active but lower priority. Closed duplicate H62 and low-priority/redundant EGFR, RB1, ERBB2 interaction, MYC broad, and interaction claims to preserve analysis capacity. Validation capacity remains 6. Best voluntary validation candidate remains H13 KEAP1-NFE2L2 after subtype and KRAS-context robustness if it remains large and precise. All conclusions are supplied-dataset associations, not causal or clinical claims."
